1 w 0.12 0.35 0.65 rg 50.00 708.00 4.00 22.00 re f
0.10 0.25 0.45 rg BT /F2 14 Tf 62.00 712.00 Td (Chapter 1: Introduction) Tj ET
1 w 0.75 0.82 0.92 RG 50.00 705.00 m 562.00 705.00 l S
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 672.00 Td (1.1 What is an Operating System?) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 653.00 Td (An Operating System \(OS\) is the fundamental system software that acts as an intermediary) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 640.50 Td (between the computer hardware and user applications. The primary goals of an operating system) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 628.00 Td (are twofold:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 611.50 Td (1. **Convenience and Abstraction:** The bare hardware consists of silicon logic gates,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 599.00 Td (registers, arithmetic-logic units, buses, memory chips, and I/O devices. Writing code to) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 586.50 Td (interface with hardware directly is tedious and error-prone. The OS abstracts these physical) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 574.00 Td (resources into clean, high-level abstractions such as processes \(abstracting the CPU\), virtual) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 561.50 Td (memory and address spaces \(abstracting physical RAM\), and files/directories \(abstracting) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 549.00 Td (non-volatile block storage\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 532.50 Td (2. **Resource Management and Protection:** In a modern computer system, multiple programs run) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 520.00 Td (concurrently. The OS arbitrates resource contention, allocates CPU time and memory fairly,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 507.50 Td (isolates processes so that a misbehaving or malicious program cannot crash other programs or) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 495.00 Td (the kernel, and enforces privilege separation between user mode and supervisor/kernel mode.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 467.50 Td (1.2 What is a Process?) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 448.50 Td (A **program** is a passive collection of instructions and static data stored on disk \(an) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 436.00 Td (executable binary file, such as an ELF binary\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 419.50 Td (A **process** is an active, running instance of a program. A process possesses:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 403.00 Td (* An isolated **virtual address space** consisting of instructions \(text\), global/static data,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 390.50 Td (a dynamically expandable heap \(via `sbrk`\), and a call stack.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 374.00 Td (* Execution context, which includes general-purpose registers \(program counter `pc`, stack) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 361.50 Td (pointer `sp`, argument and return registers\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 345.00 Td (* Operating system state, such as an entry in the Process Table, an integer Process Identifier) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 332.50 Td (\(PID\), parent/child relationships, state flags \(e.g., `RUNNABLE`, `SLEEPING`\), open file) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 320.00 Td (descriptors, and a kernel stack.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 292.50 Td (1.3 What is CPU Scheduling?) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 273.50 Td (On a computer with $N$ CPU cores and $M$ runnable processes where $M > N$ \(most commonly on) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 261.00 Td (single-core systems where $N=1$\), the hardware CPU can physically execute instructions for only) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 248.50 Td (one process at any single instant in time.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 232.00 Td (**CPU Scheduling** is the mechanism and policy by which the operating system decides which) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 219.50 Td (runnable process is awarded execution time on a CPU core, when it starts running, and when it) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 207.00 Td (must relinquish the CPU to allow another process to execute.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 179.50 Td (1.4 Why is CPU Scheduling Required?) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 160.50 Td (Without CPU scheduling:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 144.00 Td (* If a program enters an infinite compute loop \(`while\(1\);`\), the entire computer freezes) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 131.50 Td (permanently because no other process or background service can ever execute.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 115.00 Td (* When a process issues an I/O request \(such as waiting for user keyboard input or reading a) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 102.50 Td (disk block\), the CPU would sit completely idle doing zero useful work while waiting for the) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 90.00 Td (slow mechanical or electronic device.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 73.50 Td (* Interactive user programs \(such as shells, text editors, and GUIs\) would suffer unacceptable) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 61.00 Td (response lag if locked out by long-running batch calculations.) Tj ET
0.50 0.50 0.50 rg BT /F1 7.5 Tf 50 765 Td (xv6-riscv MLFQ Scheduler -- OS Project Technical Report) Tj ET
