0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 731.00 Td (hardware \(keyboard, disk, network, timer\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 714.50 Td (Processes naturally fall into two broad classifications:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 698.00 Td (1. **Interactive \(I/O-Bound\) Processes:** Characterized by very short, frequent CPU bursts) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 685.50 Td (followed by long I/O bursts. Example: `sh` \(the shell\), which wakes up, parses a command string) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 673.00 Td (\(1ms CPU burst\), and waits for the user to type again.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 656.50 Td (2. **Batch \(CPU-Bound\) Processes:** Characterized by very long, continuous CPU bursts with) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 644.00 Td (minimal I/O. Example: scientific simulators, compilers, cryptography routines.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 616.50 Td (2.4 Time Quantum and Timer Interrupts) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 597.50 Td (A **Time Quantum** \(or time slice\) is the maximum continuous duration of CPU time allocated to) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 585.00 Td (a process before it can be forcibly preempted.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 568.50 Td (Timer interrupts are periodic hardware signals delivered to the CPU by a hardware timer \(such) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 556.00 Td (as the RISC-V CLINT / ACLINT timer\). On each timer tick \(configured in xv6 via) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 543.50 Td (`w_stimecmp\(r_time\(\) + 1000000\)`\), the CPU halts user execution, saves registers, and vectors) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 531.00 Td (into kernel trap handlers. This hardware mechanism guarantees that the operating system retains) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 518.50 Td (control over the machine and prevents any user process from monopolizing the CPU indefinitely.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 491.00 Td (2.5 Classic Scheduling Algorithms) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 472.00 Td (1. **First-Come, First-Served \(FCFS\):** Non-preemptive. Processes are executed strictly in) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 459.50 Td (order of arrival. Suffers severely from the **convoy effect**, where short interactive tasks) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 447.00 Td (are trapped waiting behind a massive CPU-bound task.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 430.50 Td (2. **Round-Robin \(RR\):** Preemptive. Each process receives a fixed time slice. When the slice) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 418.00 Td (expires, the process is preempted and placed at the tail of the queue. While fair and) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 405.50 Td (starvation-free, short interactive jobs still experience significant latency if the queue is) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 393.00 Td (long.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 376.50 Td (3. **Shortest Job First \(SJF\) / Shortest Remaining Time First \(SRTF\):** Mathematically optimal) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 364.00 Td (for minimizing average turnaround time. However, it requires prior knowledge of the future) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 351.50 Td (length of each job's CPU burst, which an OS cannot know in advance.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 335.00 Td (4. **Static Priority Scheduling:** Each process has a fixed priority integer. The CPU always) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 322.50 Td (picks the highest priority runnable process. Suffers from permanent **starvation** of) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 310.00 Td (low-priority processes if high-priority processes continue arriving.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 282.50 Td (2.6 Preemptive vs. Non-Preemptive Scheduling) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 263.50 Td (* **Non-Preemptive Scheduling:** A process keeps the CPU until it voluntarily releases it,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 251.00 Td (either by terminating \(`exit\(\)`\) or blocking on an I/O request \(`sleep\(\)`\). An errant infinite) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 238.50 Td (loop freezes the system.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 222.00 Td (* **Preemptive Scheduling:** The operating system can interrupt and remove a running process) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 209.50 Td (from the CPU without the process's cooperation \(e.g., upon timer interrupt expiration or the) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 197.00 Td (arrival of a higher-priority task\). xv6 and modern operating systems are fully preemptive.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 169.50 Td (2.7 Starvation and Priority Inversion) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 150.50 Td (* **Starvation:** A situation where a runnable process waits indefinitely in the ready queue) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 138.00 Td (because higher-priority processes continually consume all available CPU time.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 121.50 Td (* **Priority Inversion:** A concurrency anomaly where a high-priority process is blocked) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 109.00 Td (waiting for a lock held by a low-priority process, while middle-priority processes run freely,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 96.50 Td (indirectly starving the high-priority process.) Tj ET
