1 w 0.12 0.35 0.65 rg 50.00 708.00 4.00 22.00 re f
0.10 0.25 0.45 rg BT /F2 14 Tf 62.00 712.00 Td (Chapter 3: MLFQ Theory and Principles) Tj ET
1 w 0.75 0.82 0.92 RG 50.00 705.00 m 562.00 705.00 l S
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 672.00 Td (3.1 Definition and Core Philosophy of MLFQ) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 653.00 Td (The **Multi-Level Feedback Queue \(MLFQ\)** scheduler was originally designed by Fernando J.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 640.50 Td (Corbat? et al. in 1962 for the Compatible Time-Sharing System \(CTSS\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 624.00 Td (The core philosophy of MLFQ is to:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 607.50 Td (> **Optimize turnaround time for long-running batch jobs while simultaneously minimizing) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 595.00 Td (response time for interactive jobs, without requiring any prior knowledge of job execution) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 582.50 Td (length.**) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 566.00 Td (MLFQ accomplishes this by observing the past behavior of a process and using that history to) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 553.50 Td (predict its future behavior -- hence the word **Feedback**.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 526.00 Td (3.2 Why MLFQ is Needed: The Problem of the Unknown Future) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 507.00 Td (In theoretical scheduling, Shortest Job First \(SJF\) achieves the lowest average waiting time.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 494.50 Td (However, in a real general-purpose OS, the kernel cannot know whether a newly launched process) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 482.00 Td (will run for 2 milliseconds \(e.g., `echo "hi"`\) or 2 hours \(e.g., rendering a 3D video\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 465.50 Td (MLFQ solves this dilemma by **assuming every new process is short and interactive until proven) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 453.00 Td (otherwise**.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 425.50 Td (3.3 Feedback-Based Dynamic Priority Adjustment) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 406.50 Td (When a job enters the system, it is granted the highest priority. If it finishes quickly \(like) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 394.00 Td (an interactive keystroke handler\), it leaves the system rapidly with near-zero latency. If it) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 381.50 Td (continues running and consuming CPU bursts, MLFQ infers that it is a compute-intensive) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 369.00 Td (background task and progressively lowers its priority level.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 341.50 Td (3.4 Multi-Level Priority Queues \(Q0 to Q3\)) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 322.50 Td (Our implementation partitions the ready processes into **four discrete priority queues**:) Tj ET
