0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 522.00 Td (If a process voluntarily releases the CPU before exhausting its allocated time quantum \(e.g.,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 509.50 Td (by sleeping for disk I/O, waiting for a console keystroke, or calling `pause\(\)`/`yield\(\)`\), it) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 497.00 Td (demonstrates that it is not monopolizing the CPU. Under our implemented MLFQ policy:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 480.50 Td (> **A process that yields or sleeps before exhausting its time slice retains its current) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 468.00 Td (priority level.**) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 451.50 Td (When it wakes up after I/O completion, it resumes at its high priority level and can respond) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 439.00 Td (immediately to the user.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 411.50 Td (3.8 The Gaming Problem and Partial Quantum Accounting) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 392.50 Td (A classic vulnerability in naive MLFQ implementations \(identified in OSTEP Chapter 8\) is) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 380.00 Td (**scheduler gaming**:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 363.50 Td (> An adversarial process could run for 99% of its quantum, issue an arbitrary I/O request \(or) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 351.00 Td (voluntary yield\) just before the quantum timer expires, retain its priority, and reset its) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 338.50 Td (quantum counter upon return. By doing this repeatedly, a CPU-bound process could monopolize 99%) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 326.00 Td (of the CPU in the highest priority queue forever!) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 309.50 Td (Our implementation prevents this vulnerability by maintaining **precise partial quantum) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 297.00 Td (accounting**:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 280.50 Td (* Each process has a field `p->current_ticks` that records cumulative ticks used in the) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 268.00 Td (current quantum.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 251.50 Td (* When a process yields or is preempted by a higher-priority process, `p->current_ticks` is) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 239.00 Td (**preserved** rather than wiped clean!) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 222.50 Td (* When the process resumes execution, it must continue consuming only its remaining ticks.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 210.00 Td (Once the sum of its partial executions reaches the queue's quantum, it is demoted.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 193.50 Td (* This adheres strictly to **OSTEP Rule 4**: *"Once a job uses up its time allotment at a) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 181.00 Td (given level \(regardless of how many times it has given up the CPU\), its priority is reduced."*) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 153.50 Td (3.9 Priority Boosting and Starvation Elimination) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 134.50 Td (If several compute-intensive processes enter the system alongside continuous interactive) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 122.00 Td (processes, the CPU-bound processes will be demoted to Q3 and could **starve** \(receive zero CPU) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 109.50 Td (time indefinitely\).) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 93.00 Td (Furthermore, if a previously compute-bound program transitions into an interactive phase, it) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 80.50 Td (would be trapped in Q3 with sluggish response times.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 64.00 Td (To solve both problems, MLFQ implements **Periodic Priority Boosting**:) Tj ET
