0.20 0.20 0.20 rg BT /F2 9.5 Tf 50.00 217.00 Td (3. Why Process IDs \(PIDs\) Remain Invariant During Process Lifetime) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 202.00 Td (A common misunderstanding in process management is confusing a **Process Table Slot** with a) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 189.50 Td (**Process ID \(PID\)**:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 173.00 Td (* **A process's PID NEVER changes while it is running.** When `mlfq_trace` spawns Worker A,) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 160.50 Td (the kernel calls `allocpid\(\)` in `kernel/proc.c`, assigning it a unique ID \(e.g., PID 4\). As) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 148.00 Td (Worker A transitions between queues \($Q0 \\rightarrow Q1 \\rightarrow Q2 \\rightarrow Q3$\), it) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 135.50 Td (remains PID 4 throughout its entire existence.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 119.00 Td (* **Why do separate test binaries show advancing PIDs?** When independent test programs) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 106.50 Td (\(`test_cpu`, `test_yield`, etc.\) are run in sequence, each test program calls `fork\(\)`, which) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 94.00 Td (advances the kernel's global monotonic `nextpid` counter \($5 \\rightarrow 6 \\rightarrow 7) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 81.50 Td (\\dots$\). Those are brand-new processes, not existing processes changing IDs.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 65.00 Td (* **Why Operating Systems increment PIDs:** Monotonically incrementing PIDs prevents the **PID) Tj ET
0.50 0.50 0.50 rg BT /F1 7.5 Tf 50 765 Td (xv6-riscv MLFQ Scheduler -- OS Project Technical Report) Tj ET
