1 w 0.12 0.35 0.65 rg 50.00 708.00 4.00 22.00 re f
0.10 0.25 0.45 rg BT /F2 14 Tf 62.00 712.00 Td (Chapter 9: Limitations and Future Improvements) Tj ET
1 w 0.75 0.82 0.92 RG 50.00 705.00 m 562.00 705.00 l S
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 672.00 Td (9.1 Static vs. Dynamic Time Quanta) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 653.00 Td (In our implementation, the time quanta \($1, 2, 4, 8$ ticks\) are hardcoded constants. While) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 640.50 Td (effective for typical workloads, high-performance operating systems \(such as Linux with CFS or) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 628.00 Td (FreeBSD with ULE\) dynamically adjust time slices based on overall system load, number of active) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 615.50 Td (threads, and target latency.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 588.00 Td (9.2 Tuning the Priority Boost Period) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 569.00 Td (Choosing `BOOST_INTERVAL` involves a fundamental trade-off:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 552.50 Td (* If `BOOST_INTERVAL` is **too short**, CPU-bound jobs spend too much time in high-priority) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 540.00 Td (queues, degrading interactive responsiveness for user-facing applications.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 523.50 Td (* If `BOOST_INTERVAL` is **too long**, starved CPU-bound jobs wait excessively, and tasks that) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 511.00 Td (transition from compute-bound to interactive suffer noticeable latency.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 483.50 Td (9.3 Multicore / Symmetric Multiprocessing \(SMP\) Challenges) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 464.50 Td (Our initial evaluation was conducted on a single core \(`CPUS=1`\). When running on multiple) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 452.00 Td (cores \(`CPUS=3` or `CPUS=8`\), several advanced considerations arise:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 435.50 Td (1. **Queue Contention:** Multiple CPUs concurrently executing `scheduler\(\)` scan the single) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 423.00 Td (global `proc[NPROC]` table, creating spinlock contention on `p->lock`.) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 406.50 Td (2. **CPU Affinity and Cache Locality:** A process may run on CPU 0 in Q0, be preempted, and) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 394.00 Td (then be scheduled on CPU 1 in Q1. Migrating across cores invalidates CPU L1/L2 caches, causing) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 381.50 Td (cache misses. Production kernels implement per-CPU runqueues with load balancing to preserve) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 369.00 Td (cache affinity.) Tj ET
0.15 0.30 0.50 rg BT /F2 11 Tf 50.00 341.50 Td (9.4 Proposed Enhancements) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 322.50 Td (1. **Decay-Based Priority \(BSD Unix style\):** Rather than periodic global boosting, gradually) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 310.00 Td (decay the CPU usage score of each process over time using an exponential moving average) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 297.50 Td (formula:) Tj ET
0.15 0.15 0.15 rg BT /F1 9 Tf 50.00 281.00 Td ($$\\text{Priority} = \\text{Base} + \\frac{\\text{CPU\\_Usage}}{2}$$) Tj ET
